"Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he buys only whole packs and expects some extras?","Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?",7,7,7,7,"Mr. Jackson has 27 students, each needing 2 glue sticks, so he needs 27 × 2 = 54 glue sticks. Each pack contains 8 glue sticks. Dividing the total needed by the pack size: 54 ÷ 8 = 6.75. Since he must purchase whole packs and needs enough to cover all students, he needs to round up to the next whole number, which is 7. Seven packs provide 7 × 8 = 56 glue sticks, leaving 2 extras.

