"Robert is building a 15 foot long, 10 foot wide, rectangular wooden fence around his garden. He needs 2 wood slats for every foot of fencing. How many wooden slats will he need?","Robert is building a 15 foot long, 10 foot wide, rectangular wooden fence around his garden. He needs 2 wood slats for every foot of fencing he builds. How many wooden slats will he need?",100,100,100,100,"First, calculate the perimeter of the rectangular garden. The formula for the perimeter of a rectangle is \(2 \times (\text{length} + \text{width})\). 

Given a length of 15 feet and a width of 10 feet:
\[
\text{Perimeter} = 2 \times (15 \, \text{ft} + 10 \, \text{ft}) = 2 \times 25 \, \text{ft} = 50 \, \text{ft}
\]

Next, determine the number of wood slats needed. Since Robert needs 2 slats per foot of fencing:
\[
\text{Number of slats} = 50 \, \text{ft} \times 2 \, \text{slats/ft} = 100 \, \text{slats}
\]

#### 100","Okay, so Robert is building this wooden fence around his garden, and I need to figure out how many wooden slats he requires. Let me try to break down the problem step by step. Hmm, let's see... 

First, the problem says it's a rectangular fence. The dimensions are 15 feet long and 10 feet wide. Wait, ""long"" and ""wide"" probably correspond to the length and width of the rectangle. So the garden is a rectangle with length 15 feet and width 10 feet. The fence goes around the perimeter of this rectangle.

Okay, so the first thing I need to calculate is the perimeter of the rectangle. The formula for the perimeter of a rectangle is 2 times (length plus width). Let me check that again... yes, because a rectangle has two lengths and two widths. So calculating perimeter: 2*(15 + 10). Let me do that. 15 + 10 is 25, multiplied by 2 gives 50 feet. So the total fencing required is 50 feet.

