"Thomas withdraws $1000 in 20 dollar bills from the bank account on a sunny day. He loses 10 bills while getting home. After that, he uses half of the remaining bills to pay for a bill. Thomas then triples his money. He then converts all his bills to 5 dollar bills. How many 5 dollar bills does he have?","Thomas withdraws $1000 in 20 dollar bills from the bank account. He loses 10 bills while getting home. After that, he uses half of the remaining bills to pay for a bill. Thomas then triples his money. He then converts all his bills to 5 dollar bills. How many 5 dollar bills does he have?",240,240,240,240,"Thomas starts with $1000 in $20 bills, which is 1000 ÷ 20 = 50 bills. He loses 10 bills, leaving 50 - 10 = 40 bills. He uses half of the remaining 40 bills (40 ÷ 2 = 20 bills) to pay for a bill, leaving 20 bills. The value of these 20 bills is 20 × $20 = $400. Tripling this amount gives $400 × 3 = $1200. Converting $1200 into $5 bills results in 1200 ÷ 5 = 240 bills.

